**FINAL DEVELOPMENT INSTRUCTION**

Prioritise a fully functional maze gameplay experience before implementing secondary features.

Do not use placeholder buttons, simulated leaderboards, hardcoded user accounts or fake Firebase functionality.

All navigation, gameplay, scoring, authentication, progression, database operations and administration features must be genuinely implemented and tested.

Build the game with a polished, relaxing presentation, highly responsive controls and an extensible architecture capable of supporting thousands of maze puzzles and a growing online player community.

## Recommended development considerations

A few important decisions will help make the build more reliable:

| Area             | Recommendation                                       |
| ---------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
| Puzzle storage   | Store maze layouts and generation seeds in Firestore |
| Maze generation  | Generate and validate puzzles before publishing      |
| Authentication   | Firebase Email/Password                              |
| Leaderboards     | Cloud Functions with server-side validation          |
| Progression      | Individual user progress documents                   |
| Daily challenges | Scheduled Cloud Functions                            |
| Administration   | Separate protected admin dashboard                   |

My strongest recommendation: build the maze generation engine and playable Classic mode first, then introduce the Firebase functionality and additional game modes. This will make it much easier to test the actual gameplay before adding the more complicated systems.

The next useful development step would be to create the complete Firebase database schema and a starter collection of 200 maze puzzles, ready for import into the application.